The relationship between counting systems and the development of high number mathematics is foundational, as counting systems provide the structural basis for representing and manipulating numbers. Early counting systems, such as numeral systems, evolved from symbolic representations of small numbers to rules for expressing larger numbers, enabling mathematical operations like addition, multiplication, and algebra [[1](https://www.britannica.com/science/numeral)]. The transition from verbal counting to symbolic numeral systems (e.g., base-10) allowed for the abstraction necessary to handle complex mathematical concepts, including exponents, real numbers, and zero [[6](http://alumni.media.mit.edu/~stefanm/society/som_final.html)]. 

Cognitive systems like the approximate number system (ANS) and parallel individuation (PI) are hypothesized to underpin symbolic number understanding, suggesting that innate numerical cognition interacts with cultural counting systems to develop mathematical abilities [[3](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27747998/)]. Early counting activities, such as one-to-one correspondence and cardinality, are critical precursors to number sense, which is essential for advanced mathematical thinking like magnitude estimation and operations [[5](https://fhsu.pressbooks.pub/ecumath/chapter/chapter-9-early-number-concepts-number-sense/)]. 

Counting systems also support foundational mathematical concepts such as place value, part-whole relationships, and equivalence, which are prerequisites for high-level mathematics like algebra and calculus [[9](https://prek-math-te.stanford.edu/counting/mathematics-counting-0)]. The developmental link between counting as a notational system and number conservation further highlights how early numerical skills evolve into abstract mathematical reasoning [[10](https://www.jstor.org/stable/1129054)]. 

